### What are the top 20 items that are reordered most frequently (display their names and product IDs)?
reordered = order_products[order_products['reordered'] == 1]
reordered = reordered.merge(products, on = 'product_id', how = 'left')
reordered_top20 = reordered.groupby(['product_id', 'product_name']).size().sort_values(ascending=False).head(20).reset_index()
reordered_top20.columns = ['product_id', 'product_name', 'times_reordered']
reordered_top20

Obviously, most frequently customers by fresh fruits and berries plus dairy (milk) since these products have a short shelf life.


### For each product, what proportion of its orders are reorders (create a table with columns for the product ID, product name, and reorder proportion)?
reorder_rate_df = (
    order_products.merge(products, on='product_id')
    .groupby(['product_id', 'product_name'])
    .agg(total_orders=('reordered', 'size'), reorder_rate=('reordered', 'mean'))
    .assign(reorder_rate=lambda x: x['reorder_rate'].round(2))
    .sort_values(by='product_id', ascending=True)
    .reset_index()
)
reorder_rate_df


